A||||2||4Banger Bandit||Odd startup problems... Can you help?
Z||000000||4Banger Bandit||07-29-99||10:47 PM||||I just picked up an '85 4-Banger with a GT nose and an auto, but I have a problem.  After buying the car and going out to start it, the car started right up, and then just died.   After that, the engine would just keep cranking, but no-go.  It seemed like it was flooded.  The guy I bought it from seemed to think it had vapor-locked.  We ended up loosening the fuel filter and the gas foamed up immediately.  He said that was a sign that it was vapor-locked, but I have no experience with that type of problem. Anyhow, the car started right up and idled fine after reconnecting the fuel filter.  I'm going to replace it tomorrow anyways, but what's going on?  <p>thanks..
Z||000001||batboy||07-30-99||05:50 AM||||Vapor locking the fuel was a problem with older vehicles with mechanical fuel pumps mounted on the engine that pulled the gasoline, especially if the fuel lines were routed near the engine and on extremely hot days. Fieros have electric fuel pumps mounted at the gas tank that push the fuel which vitually eliminates vapor lock. I doubt that the problem was vapor lock, but on the other hand, I don't know what the problem is if it wasn't.
Z||000002||DJRice||07-30-99||11:31 AM||||Definitley replace the fuel filter, but the fuel lines may be clogged or something too.  If this dude you bought it from let it sit with the hoses disconnected, they could have gotten garbage in them.  <p>The next time it turns over but wont crank, Remove the breather and see if there is fuel squirting from the injector.  If not you know  where to start looking, IF there is, then you may want to check distributor, rotor, plugs and wires, and even timing, although if it runs sometimes the timing thing shouldnt be an issue.<p>
